================= ReentrantLock Methods & Use Cases =================

1) void lock()
Use Case:
- Standard locking for critical section.
- When waiting indefinitely is acceptable.
Notes:
- Blocks until lock is available.
- Not interruptible.


2) void lockInterruptibly() throws InterruptedException
Use Case:
- When waiting thread should be cancellable.
- Useful in responsive systems (server/UI).
Notes:
- Blocks until available.
- Can be interrupted while waiting.


3) boolean tryLock()
Use Case:
- Deadlock avoidance.
- Performance-sensitive operations.
- When you don’t want to wait.
Notes:
- Returns immediately.
- true → lock acquired
- false → lock not acquired


4) boolean tryLock(long timeout, TimeUnit unit)
   throws InterruptedException
Use Case:
- Wait only for limited time.
- Prevent indefinite blocking.
- Resource pooling / distributed systems.
Notes:
- Waits up to timeout.
- Interruptible.
- Returns true/false.


5) void unlock()
Use Case:
- Releases the lock.
Best Practice:
    lock.lock();
    try {
        // critical section
    } finally {
        lock.unlock();
    }
Notes:
- Must be called by owning thread only.


6) Condition newCondition()
Use Case:
- Multiple wait-sets.
- Advanced producer-consumer.
- Replacement for wait()/notify().
Used With:
- await()
- signal()
- signalAll()


7) boolean isLocked()
Use Case:
- Monitoring / debugging.
- Check if any thread holds lock.


8) boolean isHeldByCurrentThread()
Use Case:
- Safety check before unlock().
- Debug reentrancy.


9) int getHoldCount()
Use Case:
- Check reentrancy depth.
- Debug nested locking.


10) boolean hasQueuedThreads()
Use Case:
- Detect contention.
- Check if threads are waiting.


11) int getQueueLength()
Use Case:
- Approximate number of waiting threads.
- Performance monitoring.


12) Collection<Thread> getQueuedThreads()
Use Case:
- Inspect waiting threads (debugging).


13) boolean hasWaiters(Condition condition)
Use Case:
- Check if threads waiting on specific condition.


14) int getWaitQueueLength(Condition condition)
Use Case:
- Approximate number of threads waiting on condition.

======================================================================

Reentrancy (How It Works)

In ReentrantLock:

if (currentThread == owner)
    state++;

Same thread can acquire multiple times.

state tracks hold count.

unlock() decrements state.

Lock released only when state == 0.



Lock Acquisition Flow (Exclusive Mode)

Example: ReentrantLock.lock()

Step 1: Try Fast Path
if (compareAndSetState(0, 1))

✔ If success → thread gets lock
❌ If fails → go to queue

Step 2: Add to Queue

Thread creates Node and joins tail:

... → Tail
          ↓
     New Node (CAS tail update)
Step 3: Park Thread

Thread waits using:

LockSupport.park()

Thread becomes BLOCKED.

Step 4: Unlocking

When owner calls unlock():

setState(0);
unparkSuccessor(head);

Next thread is unparked using:

LockSupport.unpark(thread);









